TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "action": "final",
  "output": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "defensive suppression of pick-and-roll (PNR fail)",
      "offensive counter strategy: pull-up jump shot after failed PNR",
      "league-wide lift consistency (proportion >0, median >2pp)"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "operational definition of PNR failure from play-by-play",
      "identification of pull-up jump shots as a distinct counter",
      "definition of unit of analysis for league-wide generalization",
      "measurement of lift relative to a baseline after failed PNR",
      "statistical test for compound hypothesis: proportion positive > 0.7 and median lift > 0.02",
      "confounding by game context (shot clock, score, defense)",
      "possession lineage and event timing dependence"
    ],
    "historical_evidence": [],
    "failed_approaches": [],
    "evidence_establishes": [],
    "evidence_does_not_establish": [
      "No prior study families were retrieved, so the available project history does not establish whether pull-up jumpers after failed pick-and-rolls provide a real league-wide counter with the claimed thresholds."
    ],
    "own_inferences": [
      "The research question, as stated, requires explicit operationalization of 'PNR fail' and 'PULL_UP' which are not uniquely determined by the data dictionary; this introduces substantial risk of measurement-driven artifacts.",
      "The compound requirement (≥70% of units positive lift and median lift >2pp) constitutes a joint test that demands careful handling of both sampling variability and clustering; a simple binomial test is likely mis-specified.",
      "Confounding by game situation (shot clock, score, defensive alignment) is likely to influence both the decision to shoot a pull-up and its expected points, making simple lift comparisons liable to spurious effect.",
      "Even with multiple seasons of data, some team-offense units may have only a modest number of failed PNR → pull-up sequences, threatening power at the unit level and inflating false negative risk for the proportion threshold."
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The exact definition of a failed pick-and-roll is not standardized; different time windows or event criteria could lead to different inclusion sets.",
      "The reliability of derived play-type possession records for classifying 'pull-up' as distinct from other jump shots (catch-and-shoot) is unknown from the data catalog alone.",
      "The appropriate baseline for lift is unclear: should it be all other possessions after a failed PNR, or possessions matched on context?",
      "The stability of the unit-level estimator across seasons and the impact of player personnel changes are uncertain."
    ],
    "historical_effect": {
      "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
      "explanation": "No relevant earlier study families were returned; the decision is based solely on the task statement and methodological assessment of the question and data."
    },
    "testability": {
      "p_testable": 0.65,
      "blocking_constraints": [
        {
          "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
          "text": "The number of failed-PNR possessions leading to a pull-up shot may be small per team-season, reducing power for unit-level lift estimation and for testing the ≥70% threshold."
        },
        {
          "code": "CONFOUNDING",
          "text": "Game-state variables (shot clock, score, defensive pressure) affect both the choice to shoot a pull-up and its success probability, so an unadjusted lift measure confounds the counter effect with situation."
        },
        {
          "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
          "text": "Classification of play types as PNR failure and as pull-up depends on derived data; misclassification error may bias lift estimates and increase false positives."
        },
        {
          "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
          "text": "The compound hypothesis tests a proportion and a median simultaneously; without appropriate joint testing, the overall false-positive rate could be inflated."
        }
      ]
    },
    "prior_work": {
      "already_answered": "unknown",
      "cited": [],
      "summary": "No earlier study families were retrieved; it is unknown whether this specific question has been investigated within the project."
    },
    "risks": [
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Small number of qualifying possessions per team-offense season undermines precise unit-level lift estimates and reduces power to detect the required proportion of positive units."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "PNR failure and pull-up classifications rely on derived play-type labels; systematic mislabeling (e.g., catch-and-shoot miscoded as pull-up, or late-PNR resets missed) could create a spurious lift signal."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "The decision to take a pull-up after a failed PNR is confounded by shot-clock time, defensive positioning, and offensive set; these unmeasured factors introduce bias of unknown direction and magnitude."
      },
      {
        "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
        "text": "The research hypothesis combines a proportion-of-units criterion and a median threshold without specifying a joint testing framework, risking a false-positive conclusion from cumulative error."
      },
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Even with full regular-season data, the power to confirm that at least 70% of units have positive lift with a median >2pp may be low, particularly if the true positive proportion is near the threshold."
      },
      {
        "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
        "text": "When comparing points per possession after a failed action, the baseline PPP may already be low; a small absolute increase could be an artifact of a bounded outcome rather than a genuine tactical counter."
      }
    ],
    "predicted_outcome": {
      "supported": 0.05,
      "rejected": 0.10,
      "untestable": 0.30,
      "artifact": 0.50,
      "uncertain": 0.05
    },
    "decision": "do_not_proceed",
    "design": {
      "estimand": "The difference in points per possession between possessions ending with a pull-up jump shot following a failed pick-and-roll and a matched set of possessions that also followed a failed pick-and-roll but ended in any other action, measured at the team-offense season level.",
      "unit": "team-offense season (e.g., 'Boston 2023-24 offense')",
      "sample": "All regular-season possessions from 2022-23 through 2025-26 in which a pick-and-roll was identified and classified as failed (no shot, assist, or drawn foul attributed to the PNR action within four seconds of the screen), excluding possessions where the shot clock was below 5 seconds at the start of the PNR. Pull-up possessions are those where the possession ended with a jump shot by the PNR ball-handler off the dribble, as labeled in the derived play-type records.",
      "power": "Estimated based on approximately 400 eligible failed-PNR possessions per team-offense season, with 10–15% leading to a pull-up. A one-sample test of proportion positive requires simulation: with 30 units, detecting a true proportion of 0.7 against a null of 0.5 with 80% power may be marginal. The compound test will reduce power further; a full simulation-based power analysis is required before proceeding.",
      "controls": "Match control possessions to pull-up possessions on shot-clock bin (0-4,5-9,...), period, score margin (leading/trailing by categories), defensive matchup quality (using nearest defender distance), and ball-handler identity via propensity score weighting to estimate the average effect of the counter.",
      "falsification": "Apply the same lift estimator to possessions after successful PNR (immediate shot from PNR action); there should be no consistent lift from subsequent pull-ups. Also test a 'placebo' counter such as a non-pull-up mid-range jumper or floater, which should not show a systematic lift if the mechanism is specific to pull-ups.",
      "multiplicity": "The null hypothesis is that the true proportion of units with positive lift is ≤0.7 or the true median lift ≤0.02 PPP. The test will use a joint permutation test: permute the counter label across possessions within each unit, recompute the proportion and median, and compare the observed pair to the permutation distribution. The decision rule uses a single p-value threshold of 0.05.",
      "decision_rule": "Reject the null if the observed (proportion positive, median lift) pair lies in the extreme tail of the permutation distribution (p < 0.05) and both observed statistics exceed the required thresholds.",
      "data_version": "NBA regular season data 2022-23 through 2025-26; derived play-type possessions from the graph database as of 2026-05-23."
    }
  }
}

